<div id="recipe-88375-notes" class="wprm-recipe-notes-container wprm-block-text-normal"><h3 class="wprm-recipe-header wprm-recipe-notes-header wprm-block-text-bold wprm-align-left wprm-header-decoration-none" style="">Notes</h3><div class="wprm-recipe-notes"><span style="display: block;"><b>Choosing the right eggplant for this recipe:</b><span style="font-weight: 400;"> The ideal type for this kebab is the long, slim variety. If your market only carries the wider globe eggplant, trim the largest center slices so they match the rest. This way everything cooks at the same time.</span></span><div class="wprm-spacer"></div>
<span style="display: block;"><span style="font-weight: 400;"><strong>*Note:</strong> If your eggplant slices are on the larger side or you are using a larger baking dish, you may run out of meat (or eggplant slices) towards the middle of the spiral. If that happens, you can use one more tomato and/or eggplant to fill the middle section.</span></span><div class="wprm-spacer"></div>
<span style="display: block;"><b>Storage:</b><span style="font-weight: 400;"> Let leftovers cool to room temperature, then store in an airtight container in the fridge for up to 4 days. Reheat in a low oven at 325&deg;F for 10-15 minutes or until heated thoroughly.</span></span><div class="wprm-spacer"></div>
<span style="display: block;"><b>Freezing: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">You can </span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">technically</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;"> freeze the baked eggplant kebab, but I find eggplant becomes mushy and releases its juices after thawing. Still, if you want to freeze, you can do so by placing the leftovers in a freezer-safe container for up to a month. You can thaw it in the fridge overnight.</span></span></div></div>
